//Arcanian Reserve Guide
//Fauna
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.00": "Galeabison Lanatus: Galeabison",
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.01": "Temperament: Docile",
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.04": "Drops:",
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.05": "Artisans:",
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.06": "Housing: Barn",
"FC.AnimalBook.Galebison.Page.07": "Lore: Galeabison lanatus, or the helmeted bison is easily recognizable by its unique horns, which are fused to create a large helmet-cap that covers the head, used in defense and intraspecific competition. Behaviorally they are similar to other bovids, herbivores living in mostly-female herds protected by a dominant male. The domestic variety is a source of milk and a beast of burden. ",

"FC.AnimalBook.Trabuul.Page.00": "Trabuul Camelovis Pruriens: Trabuul",
"FC.AnimalBook.Trabuul.Page.01": "Temperment: Hostile",
"FC.AnimalBook.Trabuul.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.Trabuul.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.Trabuul.Page.04": "Drops: Clay and Flowers",
"FC.AnimalBook.Trabuul.Page.05": "Housing: Coop",
"FC.AnimalBook.Trabuul.Page.06": "Lore: The trabuul is a small caprid endemic to Arcania's arid zone. With their fatty hump and lack of horns, they are sometimes mistaken for tiny camelids, but in fact are most closely related to sheep. The trabuul is an unexpected ecosystem engineer of the desert; they primarily feed on high-nutrient roots and fruits, digging small burrows and pits to access the former (as well as for shelter during the heat of day). Discarded trabuul burrows provide vital shelter for various other desert dwellers and their digging churns up vital early nutrients for the various plants that rely on them for seed dispersal. Some sites are popular year after year, and these so-called 'trabuul oases are precious for desert traversal, serving as both useful landmarks and crucial sources of natural food. Leftover roots, flowers, and clay from trabuul burrowing efforts range from helpful forage to valuable trade goods; they are not utilized for wool as their natural coat is short and very coarse, making it a poor material for textiles. Skittish and short-tempered, trabuul will sometimes attack humans with their sharp hooves, which males also use to fight in the breeding season.",

"FC.AnimalBook.Narubit.Page.00": "Narulagus Hydrochoeroides: Narubit",
"FC.AnimalBook.Narubit.Page.01": "Temperment: Docile",
"FC.AnimalBook.Narubit.Page.02": "Ability: Can swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.Narubit.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.Narubit.Page.04": "Drops: Narula Wool",
"FC.AnimalBook.Narubit.Page.05": "Housing: Barn",
"FC.AnimalBook.Narubit.Page.06": "Lore: Females are territorial and will compete to protect their claims and families. Their maternal instinct is strong; they will adopt orphaned kits from other tribes as well as young animals of other species. They have been known to guide lost humans to safety as well and are relatively strong swimmers. In captivity, the Narula is prized for its fur, which it naturally sheds in the warm months.",

"FC.AnimalBook.Tapi.Page.00": "Pseudotapirus Dentigrandis: Tapi",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tapi.Page.01": "Temperament: Hostile",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tapi.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tapi.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tapi.Page.04": "Drops: Stone and Winter Root",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tapi.Page.05": "Housing: Barn",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tapi.Page.06": "Lore: Pseudotapirus dentigrandis, otherwise known as the tapi, are generalist foragers which fill the niche of pigs. Though they resemble tapirs, genetic studies suggest they are more closely related to rhinos. Domestic and wild varieties can be distinguished by the latter’s black tusks.",

"FC.AnimalBook.Tuusca.Page.00": "Phacochoerus Arcanensis: Tuusca",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tuusca.Page.01": "Temperament: Hostile",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tuusca.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tuusca.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tuusca.Page.04": "Drops: Randomized Mushrooms",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tuusca.Page.05": "Housing: Barn",
"FC.AnimalBook.Tuusca.Page.06": "Lore: The Phacochoerus arcanensis, which is also known as the Tuusca, is the largest breed of boar in the world. These hogs have muscular bodies, facial tusks, and an omnivorous diet. Although they are solitary animals, they do form family units. While the females are less prone to violence, it is not advised to domesticate the Tuusca, as they tend to be aggressive and highly territorial.",

"FC.AnimalBook.FemaleMovoraptor.Page.00": "Female Movoraptor Arcagallus: Female Movoraptor",
"FC.AnimalBook.FemaleMovoraptor.Page.01": "Temperament: Hostile",
"FC.AnimalBook.FemaleMovoraptor.Page.02": "Ability: Can swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.FemaleMovoraptor.Page.03": "Feed Type: Carnivore Feed(Uses a feeding path)",
"FC.AnimalBook.FemaleMovoraptor.Page.04": "Drops: Movoraptor Eggs",
"FC.AnimalBook.FemaleMovoraptor.Page.05": "Housing: Barn",
"FC.AnimalBook.FemaleMovoraptor.Page.06": "Lore: The Movoraptor, also affectionately called the dino chicken, are omnivorous and are highly territorial and often aggressive, however they form tight-knit all female family units. The oldest female in the group is the boss and the chicks are usually equals until the largest takes second in command. Once a formidable predator in the Arcanian island system. However, the arrival of humans to Arcania significantly shifted the relationship between these raptors and the land, as well as its fauna. Through generations of effort, the Movoraptor was domesticated and trained to protect and care for its human counterparts. Due to their territorial instincts, female raptors often adopt groups of humans as their own offspring. In rare cases, they may select a human as a lifelong companion.Selective breeding has also transformed the reproductive process of the Movoraptors. Originally a species that relied on sexual reproduction, females have developed the ability to reproduce asexually through a process known as parthenogenesis. As a result, a male Movoraptor is produced in only two out of every twenty eggs laid. An interesting occurance, however, is when a male and female movoraptor actually reproduce asexually. While this is extremely rare, a larger genetically prehistoric variation of movoraptor can hatch.",

"FC.AnimalBook.MaleMovoraptorMovoraptor.Page.00": "Male Movoraptor Arcagallus: Male Movoraptor",
"FC.AnimalBook.MaleMovoraptorMovoraptor.Page.01": "Temperament: Hostile",
"FC.AnimalBook.MaleMovoraptorMovoraptor.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.MaleMovoraptorMovoraptor.Page.03": "Feed Type: Carnivore Feed(Uses a feeding path)",
"FC.AnimalBook.MaleMovoraptorMovoraptor.Page.04": "Drops: Movoraptor Feather",
"FC.AnimalBook.MaleMovoraptorMovoraptor.Page.05": "Housing: Barn",
"FC.AnimalBook.MaleMovoraptorMovoraptor.Page.06": "Lore: In modern times, male hatchlings are considered a genetic anomaly and seldom make it to adulthood; young Movoraptors will sometimes kill and cannibalize their smaller siblings to eliminate competition.  As the male chicks tend to be significantly smaller than females, they are frequently eaten by their larger sisters. Those that survive generally do so by being found and recruited by adult males, who are otherwise not allowed to raise chicks.The dynamic between female and male Movoraptors is a complex one. While females group up units of five to ten; the males tend to group in pairs of two to four. However, both males and females operate under the control of analpha female, who tends to be the oldest and strongest female among the flock. Male raptors will live at a distance from the females, only approaching to scavenge, recruit male hatchlings, and attempt to mate.",

"FC.AnimalBook.DeaBird.Page.00": "Necroavis Dea: Dea Bird",
"FC.AnimalBook.DeaBird.Page.01": "Temperament: Docile",
"FC.AnimalBook.DeaBird.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.DeaBird.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.DeaBird.Page.04": "Drops: Bone Fragments and Dea Eggs",
"FC.AnimalBook.DeaBird.Page.05": "Housing: Coop",
"FC.AnimalBook.DeaBird.Page.06": "Lore: Necroavis dea, otherwise known as the dea bird or death bird, is one of the smallest and most socially inclined cathartidae of Arcania. Flying in flocks of three, these birds are scavengers and feed on carrion, but will also eat several foul-smelling plants such as the luna fly bloom and the rot lily. Unlike its larger counterparts, the Dea bird tends to approach humans for social interaction. A act that most Arcanians consider a bad omen.",

"FC.AnimalBook.Raphus.Page.00": "Raphus Arcanensis: Raphus Bird",
"FC.AnimalBook.Raphus.Page.01": "Temperament: Docile",
"FC.AnimalBook.Raphus.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.Raphus.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.Raphus.Page.04": "Drops: Raphus Egg",
"FC.AnimalBook.Raphus.Page.05": "Housing: Barn",
"FC.AnimalBook.Raphus.Page.06": "Lore:Raphus arcanensis is the largest of the still-extant Fern Islands Raphus species; the extinct Great Raphus was slightly larger but lighter. Adapted to live alongside Arcania’s predators, they are more skittish, faster, and stronger than most other Raphus species. As with many other Raphus’s, human hunting has damaged their population, and they are a protected species as such.",

"FC.AnimalBook.Quillback.Page.00": "Arcoendou Foetidus: Quillback Skunk",
"FC.AnimalBook.Quillback.Page.01": "Temperament: Docile",
"FC.AnimalBook.Quillback.Page.02": "Ability: Can't swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.Quillback.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.Quillback.Page.04": "Drops: Clumps of Fiber and Various berries.",
"FC.AnimalBook.Quillback.Page.05": "Housing: Coop",
"FC.AnimalBook.Quillback.Page.06": "Lore: Confusingly, the Quillback Skunk is not a skunk, but a porcupine, and some researchers prefer the common names skunk porcupine or Arcanian skunk coendou for this reason. However, quillback skunk remains more common and is generally considered a better translation of it Arcanian name. Like most of its relatives the quillback skunk is a forest-dwelling rodent that feeds mainly on berries and shrubs, though it is a relatively terrestrial porcupine. They get their name both from the bright streak of quills on top of their body, and from their smell, which is constant and very strong, deterring predators and attracting certain insects which the quillback also eats. Fully mature quillbacks are relatively solitary; males live alone except during the mating season, as do females without young. Babies are typically born one or two every year, however young quillbacks will remain with their mother for over a year, until their younger siblings are near-weaned, and help provide food to their mother during the nursing period; the survival rate of kits with older siblings is much higher than for those without. Unafraid of humans, the quillback is easily tamed and a semi-domestic population exists; however due in large part to their strong scent, they are one of the rarest domestics of Arcania, only raised by a few small villages in their native range. An unusual form of livestock guardian, they are kept on the village outskirts where their smell deters predators. Historically, some villages also harvested the scent glands to create a form of predator repellent for hunters and travelers; in modern times there is niche interest in them within the perfume industry.",

"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab.Page.00": "Lacustrocancer Pseudopagurus: Giant Grave Crab",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab.Page.01": "Temperament: Hostile",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab.Page.02": "Ability: Can swim",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab.Page.03": "Feed Type: Hay",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab.Page.04": "Drops: GraveCrab Eggs and Shells",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab.Page.05": "Housing: Coop",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab.Page.06": "Lore: Initially believed to be freshwater hermit crabs, grave crabs are in fact a unique crustacean lineage, and unlike hermit crabs the shell is a natural part of their exoskeleton. They primarily reside deep in Arcania's lakes, where they serve a key role in the ecosystem; namely, they subsist solely off of carrion from sunken corpses, particularly of large animals such as otters, movoraptors, and the Arcanian desmostylian. Observations of grave crab feeding sites have been compared to marine whalefalls. Like the gravefish they bear cultural associations with death, but those associations are significantly more negative and the consumption of grave crabs is considered taboo. Adults can grow very large, but as with many crustaceans the leading cause of death is molting complications, and grave crabs are known to engage in cannibalism.",

"FC.AnimalBook.RaccoonDog.Page.00": "Arcacyon Viverrinus: Raccoon Dog",
"FC.AnimalBook.RaccoonDog.Page.01": "Housing: Farm House",
"FC.AnimalBook.RaccoonDog.Page.02": "Lore: The Arcanian raccoon dog is, in fact, neither a raccoon nor a dog! Studies of its genes and physiology have confirmed that its closest relatives are not dogs but bears, though debate about whether it is a true ursid or not continues. Some have proposed new common names to reflect this discovery, but as it is best known as the raccoon dog that is how we will refer to it. How this creature first came to Arcania and into contact with the native people is unclear, but it has been domesticted for centuries. The raccoon dog is generally used in hunting; though it is readily outsped by true hounds, its talent is in its bulk and natural ambush abilities.  In traditional Arcanian hunting a well-trained raccoon dog will flush prey out of the bush to where the hunter can more easily target it, help take down larger prey, and deter potential threats to the hunters such as durrahs. Highly loyal and with a powerful bite force, they will guard their human companions with their lives, though it can be difficult to retrieve carcasses that they get ahold of. Females with cubs can be aggressive and great care must be taken in breeding them. The vocalizations of the raccoon dog can be intimidating to those who haven't heard them before.",

"FC.AnimalBook.Shevit.Page.00": "Arcanomys Lingua: Shevit",
"FC.AnimalBook.Shevit.Page.01": "Housing: Farm House",
"FC.AnimalBook.Shevit.Page.02": "Lore: Arcanomys lingua is the size of a cat, the shevit is a large rodent native to Arcania. These curious rodents are highly intelligent, with some demonstrating levels of intelligence similar to three-year-old children. They have highly-developed vocal folds and can mimic a wide variety of sounds, including human speech, a trait which often startles visitors to the island system.",

"FC.AnimalBook.Miniphant.Page.00": "Gotoroloxodon Minimus: Miniphant",
"FC.AnimalBook.Miniphant.Page.01": "Housing: Farm House",
"FC.AnimalBook.Miniphant.Page.02": "Lore: Gotoroloxodon minimus is the smallest known true elephants, and one of the smallest known loxodonts, the miniphant is a marvel of evolution. Genetic and fossil evidence indicates that it and the Gotoran war elephant (G. ares) share a common ancestor, the now extinct Gotoran coastal pygmy elephant (G. lanceum). Where the war elephant was deliberately bred for larger size and increased aggression, however, the ancestral population that arrived on Arcania faced a much different challenge. The larger mammal niches were occupied, and Arcania's predators were well-suited to hunt them at their current size. Interspecific competition and the island's limited resources prevented them from becoming larger, so instead, they rapidly shrank. The resultant elephant is one of the smallest mammals on the island, but the small size allows them to avoid predation. They live relatively solitary lives, and are important dispersers of low-lying fruit. The miniphant has been a popular pet in Arcania for centuries, and is slowly gaining popularity among exotic pet enthusiasts. As with most pachyderms, they are semi-aquatic.",

"FC.AnimalBook.GuineaDog.Page.00": "Arcanomys Minimus canis: Guinea Dog",
"FC.AnimalBook.GuineaDog.Page.01": "Housing: Farm House",
"FC.AnimalBook.GuineaDog.Page.02": "Lore: Arcavia minimus canis (guinea dog) is the toy dog variation of the Arcanomys lingua (Racoon dog); it was originally bred for flushing out the burrows of rodents and rabbits and were most commonly used as a method of protecting crop feilds instead of using animal repellents. It wasn't until animals repellents became more affordable, did the Guinea dog graduate from being crop protectors to lap dogs. While loyal and docile, these dogs are prone to developing behavioral problems associated with poor enrichment and excess energy.",

//Fish
"FC.AnimalBook.BoneyHeadedGraveFish.Page.00": "Osteoapsis Thanatos: Boney Headed Grave Fish",
"FC.AnimalBook.BoneyHeadedGraveFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.01% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.BoneyHeadedGraveFish.Page.02": "Lore: Immediately recognizable by osteoderms that resemble human bone, it is little surprise that the gravefish is considered to lead souls to the afterlife in Arcanian culture. Some communities, particularly more inland ones, hold superstitions around it and thus concerns exist about overhunting for rituals or by villagers who see them as an omen. However, the gravefish is not a threat to humans; rather much like groupers and sharks they are often surprisingly friendly. They can grow up to the size of larger hammerhead sharks, and reproduce slowly.",

"FC.AnimalBook.IficusFish.Page.00": "Ificius Felix: Ificius Fish",
"FC.AnimalBook.IficusFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.30% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.IficusFish.Page.02": "Lore: Found mainly in brackish environments, the Ificius is a shy fish that mainly preys on smaller fish and shrimp. Catching an Ificius is considered a sign of good fortune and so is sometimes utilized in a delicacy at weddings. If unable to flee, they will bite; their bites are too small to pose immediate danger to most humans, however like any injury bites should be cleaned and treated promptly to prevent infection.",

"FC.AnimalBook.DancerFish.Page.00": "Acanthurus Saltator: Dancer Tang",
"FC.AnimalBook.DancerFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.25%",
"FC.AnimalBook.DancerFish.Page.02": "Lore: A dancer fish endemic to Arcania's reefs, the dancer's vibrant colors, ornate fins, and graceful underwater movements have charmed many, to the point that concerns exist about poaching for the pet trade. Although not common in aquaria, they are a popular sight where exhibited. As with most tangs they feed primarily on macroalgae and can be aggressively territorial.",

"FC.AnimalBook.RiverBedJelly.Page.00": "Arcacnida Caerulius: Riverbed Jellyfish",
"FC.AnimalBook.RiverBedJelly.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.15% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.RiverBedJelly.Page.02": "Lore: A distant relative of the lunaloo jelly, the Arcanian riverbed jelly is a freshwater species of jelly endemic to Arcania's rivers and lakes, though they are more common in the former. Their tentacles bear specially adapted adhesive discs that allow them to attech to aquatic plants and algae; from their they direct their bell towards the current to capture the plantonic organisms on which they feed. Although vibrantly colored, they are not dangerous; their venom is too mild to affect humans, though there has been talk of studies on its potential as an antiparasitic.",

"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab2.Page.00": "Lacustrocancer Pseudopagurus minimus: Adolescent Grave Crab",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab2.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.40% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.GraveCrab2.Page.02": "Lore: Without a hard shell, the infant grave crab is particularly vulnerable right after it hatches. It can take up to three days for a newly hatched grave crab to start developing its protective, hermit-like shell. Although their diet resembles that of the adults, these unprotected baby crabs will seek hiding spots until they have the opportunity to feed on carrion.",

"FC.AnimalBook.GulpFish.Page.00": "Pericugnathus Lacustrus: Gulp Fish",
"FC.AnimalBook.GulpFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.10% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.GulpFish.Page.02": "Lore: The gulpfish is a freshwater ambush predator found in Arcania's rivers and lakes. Their feeind strategy is to hide in the mud and wait for prey to approach, then snap it up in their powerful jaws. Growing up to the size of large dogs, there are at least anecdotal accounts of gulpfish taking off the feet of careless waders, but their primary prey seems to be small graves crabs and fish.",

"FC.AnimalBook.LeechFish.Page.00": "Pseudopetromyzon Sanguiphagus: Leechie or Leech Fish",
"FC.AnimalBook.LeechFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.02% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.LeechFish.Page.02": "Lore: Although it shares remarkable similarities to the better-known lampreys, the leechie fish is a ray-finned fish which convergently evolved a similar lifestyle. Unsurprisingly, they feed by latching onto prey and feed on their blood. Although such predation is rarely directly fatal, swift medical intervention is recommended should one become attached.",

"FC.AnimalBook.SharkFish.Page.00": "Sociosqualus lacustris: Minima Shark",
"FC.AnimalBook.SharkFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.20% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.SharkFish.Page.02": "Lore: The minima shark, also known as the lytel, is a very small requiem shark found in Arcania's freshwater rivers and lakes. No bigger than a piranha, these sharks exist in schools of 10-20 for protection and hunting, using their combined numbers to overwhelm large prey. Schools form in early infancy from pups born around roughly the time and area, and remain together for life. Individual sharks lacking a school are extremely cautious hunters, and prone to stess-borne illness.",

"FC.AnimalBook.SwampFish.Page.00": "Digitimordax viredans: Arcanian Green Frogfish",
"FC.AnimalBook.SwampFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.16% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.SwampFish.Page.02": "Lore: A small, voracious ambush predator, the arcanian frogfish is a species of reef anglerfish endemic to the island's coasts, which moves by 'crawling' with its strong pectoral fins. Its color comes from symbiotic algae that allow it to easily blend into its environment. Although known to attack humans, the frogfish rarely goes after prey larger than itself, and such attacks are thought to be defensive in nature. Unusually for its group its antenna is greatly reduced and appears to play no role in hunting.",

"FC.AnimalBook.PufferCousin.Page.00": "Lunamola elegans: Potbelly Moon Fish",
"FC.AnimalBook.PufferCousin.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.5% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.PufferCousin.Page.02": "Lore: Distantly related to pufferfish and molas, the potbelly moonfish is a reef fish that feeds on hard-bodied invertebrates. Its scales are prized for their beauty and are highly sought after in both traditional Arcanian and mainstream jewelry. Because it is not an abundant fish, this has led to concerns about overfishing, but the population appears stable for now.",

"FC.AnimalBook.ArcaAxolPink.Page.00": "Pink Ambystoma arcanensis: Pink Fanged Axolotl",
"FC.AnimalBook.ArcaAxolPink.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.2% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.ArcaAxolPink.Page.02": "Lore: The Arcanian axolotl is a neotenic tiger salamander  found in many freshwater environments throughout the island; as with all neotenic salamanders it lives its entire life in the gilled larval stage. They are voracious predators, and will attempt to hunt creatures far larger than themselves. Despite this, Arcanians have long kept them as pets. They are beloved for their feisty attitude, and prized for their effectiveness in ridding ponds of mosquito larvae and other potentially dangerous parasites. Other common names include the fanged axolotl, Arcanian tiger, and Sabre-Toothed Tiger Salamander.",

"FC.AnimalBook.ArcaAxolBlue.Page.00": "Blue Ambystoma arcanensis: Blue Fanged Axolotl",
"FC.AnimalBook.ArcaAxolBlue.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.2% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.ArcaAxolBlue.Page.02": "Lore: The Arcanian axolotl is a neotenic tiger salamander  found in many freshwater environments throughout the island; as with all neotenic salamanders it lives its entire life in the gilled larval stage. They are voracious predators, and will attempt to hunt creatures far larger than themselves. Despite this, Arcanians have long kept them as pets. They are beloved for their feisty attitude, and prized for their effectiveness in ridding ponds of mosquito larvae and other potentially dangerous parasites. Other common names include the fanged axolotl, Arcanian tiger, and Sabre-Toothed Tiger Salamander.",

"FC.AnimalBook.SpikedSeaHorse.Page.00": "Hippocampus venenosus: Red-Spiked Seahorse",
"FC.AnimalBook.SpikedSeaHorse.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.6% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.SpikedSeaHorse.Page.02": "Lore: The red-spiked seahorse is endemic to Arcania and highly prized for its stunning colors. However, few aquaria or hobbyists keep them, and poaching attempts are often quickly ended, due to this seahorse having uniquely evolved a potent venom, delivered by the prominent red spines. This venom is deadly without swift medical intervention; survivors report a brief sensation of severe pain, followed by dizzyness and paralysis. Although difficult to keep for this reason, the venom is of medical interest, as it can be processed to create powerful painkillers and psychoactives. Interestingly, juveniles lack venom, only beginning to produce it near maturity.",

"FC.AnimalBook.ShelledSquid.Page.00": "Arcateuthis argonautus: Helmeted Squid",
"FC.AnimalBook.ShelledSquid.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.03% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.ShelledSquid.Page.02": "Lore: This peculiar squid is unique in that it appears to possess an external shell; however, this shell is in fact a calcitic eggcase similar to that of argonaut octopuses. The female creates an egg case shortly after achieving sufficiently large size to breed; the case then provides protection for the female and her eggs until shortly before hatching, when the female abandons it, leaving it to sink to the ocean floor and be consumed by the larvae. Post-abandonment females are highly vulnerable, but if they survive will create a new egg case after 2-3 weeks and begin the cycle anew. Males are tiny and seek out females, hiding in the egg case and eating scraps of the female's prey. Helmeted squid can tuck their entire body into the egg case, and will do so if threatened.",

"FC.AnimalBook.GummigusFish.Page.00": "Risiopisces didentes: Gummigus Fish",
"FC.AnimalBook.GummigusFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.04% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.GummigusFish.Page.02": "Lore: The gummigus is a nimble swimmer that prefers rocky deepwater environments. Despite the strong emotions its seemingly impish expression tends to evoke, little is known about this enigmatic fish. Preliminary molecular work indicates it may be distantly related to the better-known crimsonfishes.",

"FC.AnimalBook.SeaSnake.Page.00": "Arcanophis maritimus: Arcanian Sea Snake",
"FC.AnimalBook.SeaSnake.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.10% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.SeaSnake.Page.02": "Lore: The Arcanian Sea Snake is the smallest member of the genus Arcanophis, and the only fully marine snake native to Arcania. Highly vulnerable in the water column, the sea snake tends to inhabit the empty shells of large molluscs, such as nautili and sea snails, which it utilizes for both protection and to ambush prey. They must leave the shell every few hours for air, and risk losing their shells to other snakes and large hermit crabs when doing so; the greatest risk is during the breeding season, when a snake may leave its shell for hours or days at a time to mate or give birth. The Arcanian sea snake is venomous; although its venom is relatively mild and ulikely to be fatal, as with any snake bite swift medical intervention is recommended.",

"FC.AnimalBook.UncleCrimson.Page.00": "Coccinichthys arcanensis: Arcanian Crimsonfish",
"FC.AnimalBook.UncleCrimson.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.02% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.UncleCrimson.Page.02": "Lore: Sometimes colloquially called the 'uncle crimsonfish' as it has been jokingly said to resemble the 'weird uncle' of the better known Gem Sea crimsonfish, the Arcanian crimsonfish is only known to lay eggs around the shores of Arcania. Much like its better known sister species, it is popular among sport fishers as a challenging catch.",

"FC.AnimalBook.WoodwindFish.Page.00": "Macrorhamphosus arcanensis: Woodwind Fish",
"FC.AnimalBook.WoodwindFish.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.7% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.WoodwindFish.Page.02": "Lore: TBW",

"FC.AnimalBook.WoodpeckerBarnacle.Page.00": "Woodpecker Barnacle",
"FC.AnimalBook.WoodpeckerBarnacle.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.6% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.WoodpeckerBarnacle.Page.02": "Lore: TBW",

"FC.AnimalBook.ArcanianFeatherStar.Page.00": "Arcanian Feather Star",
"FC.AnimalBook.ArcanianFeatherStar.Page.01": "Seller: Arcanian Hotline 0.5% Chance",
"FC.AnimalBook.ArcanianFeatherStar.Page.02": "Lore: TBW",
